00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:27,160
about today is interest rates.

31
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,160
Interest rates.

32
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:31,160
So, the percent that you earn on

33
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:33,160
a loan or that you pay on a

34
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:37,160
loan, depending on what side of it you are.

35
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:41,160
And interest rates go back thousands of years.

36
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,160
It's an old idea.

37
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:51,160
Typically, it's a few percent a year, right?

38
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:52,660
The first question we want to

39
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:54,160
try to think about is, what

40
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:56,160
explains that?

41
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:57,160
Why is it a few percent a year?

42
00:01:57,160 --> 00:01:58,160
And why not something completely different?

43
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:08,160
And why is it even a positive number?

44
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:12,160
Ever think of negative interest rates?

45
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:15,160
Well, these are basic questions.

46
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:19,160
So, I wanted to start with the history of thought.

47
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:26,160
And an economist from the 19th century,

48
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:29,160
Eugen von Boehm,

49
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:33,660
who wrote a book on the

50
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:38,160
theory of interest in the late 19th

51
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:39,160
century.

52
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:44,160
Actually, it was 1884.

53
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:48,160
And he has a long, very

54
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:52,160
verbose account of what causes interest

55
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,160
rates.

56
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:57,160
But basically, he came up with three explanations.

57
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:59,160
Why is the interest rate so high?

58
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:01,660
Well, the interest rate is something

59
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:04,160
like 5%, or 3%, or 7%,

60
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:07,160
or something in that range.

61
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:10,160
And he said there's really three causes.

62
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:13,160
One of them is technical progress.

63
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:15,660
That as the economy gets

64
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,160
more and more scientific information

65
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:21,160
about how to do things, things get more productive.

66
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:23,660
So, maybe the 3% or the 5%,

67
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,160
whatever it is, is the rate of

68
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:27,160
technical progress.

69
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:33,160
And that's how fast technology is improving.

70
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:35,660
But that's not the only

71
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:38,160
cause that von Boehm talked about.

72
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:45,160
Another one was advantages to roundaboutness.

73
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:48,160
That must be some translation from his German.

74
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:53,160
But the idea is that more roundabout production is more

75
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:54,160
productive.

76
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:55,160
This isn't technical progress.

77
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:57,160
It's more about the time.

78
00:03:57,160 --> 00:03:58,660
And if someone can ask

79
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:00,160
you to make something directly right

80
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:02,160
now, you've got to use

81
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,160
the simplest and the most direct

82
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:05,160
way to do it, if you're

83
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:06,160
going to do it right now.

84
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,660
But if you have time, you

85
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:09,160
can do a more roundabout way.

86
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:11,160
You can make tools first and

87
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:13,160
do something else that makes you a

88
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:16,159
more efficient producer of this.

89
00:04:16,159 --> 00:04:18,159
And so, maybe the interest rate

90
00:04:18,159 --> 00:04:20,159
is a measure of the advantages

91
00:04:20,159 --> 00:04:23,159
to roundaboutness.

92
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:27,160
And the other thing that Mark gave is time preference.

93
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:30,160
That people just prefer the present over the future.

94
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:32,160
They're impatient.

95
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:34,160
This is behavioral economics, I suppose.

96
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:36,160
This is psychology.

97
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:38,160
That, you know, you've got a

98
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:40,160
box of candy sitting there, and

99
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:41,660
you're looking at it, and

100
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:43,160
you're saying, well, I should really

101
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:45,160
enjoy that next year.

102
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:48,160
Maybe it would spoil by next year, next month.

103
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:50,160
But somehow you don't.

104
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:53,160
You have an impulse to consume now.

105
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:57,160
And the interest rate is the rate of time preference.

106
00:04:57,160 --> 00:04:58,660
That maybe people are, you

107
00:04:58,660 --> 00:05:00,160
know, why is the interest rate

108
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:05,160
It's because people are 5% happier to get something now

109
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:08,160
than to get it in the future.

110
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:13,160
So, he left that train of thought for us.

111
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:16,160
This was not mathematical economics.

112
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:21,160
It was literary economics.

113
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,160
But the next person I want to

114
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:33,160
mention in the history of thought is Irving Fisher, who

115
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:36,160
was a professor at Yale University.

116
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:42,160
But he wrote a book

117
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:48,160
in 1930 called Theory of Interest.

118
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:53,160
And it is the all-time classic.

119
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:58,160
I think, on this topic.

120
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:03,160
So, Irving Fisher is, he's talked about in your textbook

121
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:08,160
by Fibozzi, and talked about in many textbooks.

122
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:11,160
He graduated from Yale.

123
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:13,160
I mentioned this, since you're Yale undergrads.

124
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:15,160
He was a Yale undergrad.

125
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:18,160
Graduated, maybe it was in 1885.

126
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,160
And he got the first economics

127
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:22,160
PhD at Yale University in the

128
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:25,160
1890s.

129
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:26,660
And he just stayed here

130
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:28,160
in New Haven all his life.

131
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:30,660
And if you were living in

132
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:33,160
New Haven in the early 20th century,

133
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:37,160
you'd know him, because he was a jogger.

134
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:39,160
Nobody else jogged.

135
00:06:39,160 --> 00:06:41,160
He would exercise and run around

136
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:43,160
campus, so everyone would see him.

137
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:46,160
In the 19-teens or 20s, nobody did that.

138
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,160
But he did.

139
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,160
He was a health nut, among other things.

140
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:52,160
And he was a great guy.

141
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:53,660
But I'm not going to talk,

142
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:55,160
I could talk a lot about him.

143
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:56,160
He's a fascinating guy.

144
00:06:56,160 --> 00:06:58,160
I'll tell you one more story about him.

145
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,160
He would invite students to his house for dinner.

146
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:03,160
And he would explain to

147
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:05,160
