00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:01,160
So, well, what your author,

171
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:05,160
your textbook author, Fibozzi, emphasizes

172
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:07,660
for a theory of interest is something

173
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:10,160
that came from Fisher, but it's very simple.

174
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:13,660
And he says, the interest rate, and

175
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:17,160
this is Fibozzi's distillation of Irving Fisher,

176
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:22,160
he says, the interest rate is the intersection

177
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:27,160
of a supply and demand curve for savings.

178
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:29,160
So, I'm going to put

179
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:31,160
saving, or S, on this axis.

180
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:34,159
And on this axis, I'm going to

181
00:08:34,159 --> 00:08:37,159
put the interest rate, call that R.

182
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:40,159
I don't know why we commonly use R for interest.

183
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:42,659
It's not the first letter, it's

184
00:08:42,659 --> 00:08:45,160
in the middle of the word.

185
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:48,160
And the interest rate is the supply of saving

186
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:51,160
at any time that people then wish to put in

187
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:55,160
the bank or someplace else to earn interest.

188
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:57,660
And the theory is that the higher

189
00:08:57,660 --> 00:09:00,160
the interest rate, the more people will save.

190
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:03,160
So, we have an upward-sloping supply curve.

191
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:06,160
Now, this S means supply, whereas

192
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:09,160
this S down here means saving.

193
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:15,160
And then there's a demand for investment capital.

194
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:17,160
So, the more people put

195
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:19,160
in, the lower the interest rate.

196
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:23,160
So, this S gives out your saving to businesses.

197
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:24,660
And the businesses want to

198
00:09:24,660 --> 00:09:26,160
know what the interest rate is.

199
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:31,160
The lower the interest rate, the more they'll demand.

200
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:37,160
So, we have a demand curve for saving.

201
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:39,660
And then the intersection of

202
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:42,160
the two is the interest rate.

203
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:43,660
Well, it gives the interest rate on this axis,

204
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:45,160
and the amount of saving on the other axis.

205
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:49,160
So, that's what Fabozzi covers in your text.

206
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:52,160
But I wanted to go back to another diagram

